  "_layout": "Matches main.gd's SEQUEL_LAYOUT (x/y here are screen-space — y grows DOWNWARD, the opposite sign convention from SEQUEL_LAYOUT's Vector2s, same inversion every other campaign's pair of layouts already has; not algebraically derived from those values, just kept in the same relative arrangement — see test_sequel_era_hyperlane_graph_is_planar). Five planets, ten lane nodes (2026-09-12 revision, up from the original five-lane pentagon): Starkiller Base (First Order home, top-left) - Ajan Kloss (top-middle) - Crait (Resistance home, bottom-middle) - Takodana (bottom-left) - Jakku (middle-left) - back to Starkiller Base is still the outer loop, but Crait-Ajan Kloss is now a THREE-node arc swinging out to the right, around the galactic core, instead of a single node cutting straight through the middle — the direct route and the arc occupy disjoint x ranges so this can't cross anything. Three more lanes were added on top of the loop, each starting or ending at a planet (or, for the arc bypass, a lane node) the loop already touches, which is exactly what keeps a hand-placed addition planar for free (a shared endpoint always exempts a segment pair from the crossing check): Crait-Jakku (a lower-left bypass, swinging below/outside Takodana), a second, outer Starkiller Base-Jakku route (swinging further left/out from the direct one — Starkiller Base and Jakku are connected two different ways on purpose, not a mistake), and a second arc route between the arc's own two end nodes (crait_ajankloss_space_1, closest to Crait, and crait_ajankloss_space_3, closest to Ajan Kloss) via one more node bulging further right than the first arc's middle node — two genuinely separate right-side routes between Crait and Ajan Kloss now, so a defending fleet can't be funnelled through a single chokepoint.",
